035,0.056,0.047,0.048,0.035,0.055,0.035,0.053,0.044,0.058,0.058,0.089,0.071,0.106,0.063,0.103,0.089,0.046,0.098,0.086,0.061,0.081,0.106,0.078,0.121,0.095,0.045,0.055,0.068,0.063,0.056,0.041,0.085,0.027,0.047,0.044,0.054,0.088,0.058,0.063,0.105,0.094,0.107,0.061,0.1,0.062,0.069,0.048,0.039,0.073,0.062,0.033,0.049,0.069,0.045,0.056,0.059,0.118,0.09,0.094,0.072,0.082,0.065,0.082,0.069,0.038,0.081,0.072,0.064,0.079,0.087,0.1,0.076,0.033,0.024,0.028,0.05,0.042,0.081,0.047,0.059,0.045,0.078,0.106,0.062,0.072,0.062,0.052,0.085,0.087,0.068,0.067,0.031,0.049,0.024,0.045,0.083,0.014,0.029,0.049,0.066,0.055,0.056,0.059,0.113,0.079,0.024,0.106,0.069,0.05,0.09,0.04,0.106,0.063,0.045,0.079,0.018,0.017,0.077,0.102,0.104,0.082,0.073,0.068,0.097,0.075,0.077,0.082,0.042,0.012,0.042,0.07,0.094,0.083,0.058,0.033,0.079,0.061,0.071,0.06,0.05,0.049,0.043,0.06,0.035,0.045,0.062,0.097,0.119,0.035,0.032,0.069,0.068,0.067,0.085,0.068,0.065,0.064,0.063,0.053,0.042,0.077,0.109,0.064,0.032,0.064,0.035,0.043,0.033,0.06,0.059,0.058]}},"alerts":[{"event":"Hydrologic Outlook","headline":"Hydrologic Outlook issued September 27 at 1:01PM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"ESFGJT\n\nStrong southwesterly winds ahead of a closed low centered over\nCalifornia will pull tropical moisture from Tropical Storm Odalys and\nHurricane Polo towards eastern Utah and western Colorado, with\natmospheric moisture peaking in our area on Monday. The northerly\nextent of the expected excessive rainfall poses a threat to our\nsouthern San Juan river basins. Current forecasts suggest 1 to 4\ninches of rain Monday morning through early Wednesday. At face value,\nrivers and tributaries will see a rise due to the forecast rainfall.\nHowever, localized heavier amounts could result in flooding across\nthe southern San Juan foothills and communities further downstream.\n\nRivers have responded rapidly to recent rainfall due to saturated\nsoils and increased runoff potential. Thus, prolonged periods of\nadditional rainfall over these regions would trigger a similar\nresponse. The current precipitation forecast suggests larger peaks in\nseveral creeks and rivers than what has been recently observed.\nHowever, high end probabilistic rainfall amounts (i.e. above 90th\npercentile) may lead to rivers exceeding action stage to minor\nflooding. Most probable basins to see significant rises include; the\nRio Blanco, San Juan, Piedra, Los Pinos/Vallecito Creek, and the\nAnimas River Basins.\n\nConvective storms and periods of heavy rainfall fed from said\ntropical moisture could result in localized flooding impacts across\nthe CWA, however, confidence decreases rapidly north of the San\nJuans. Confidence has increased enough for a Flood Watch to be issued\nfor southern portions of the forecast area valid from 6am Monday\nthrough 6am Wednesday. Continue to monitor the latest forecasts\nespecially for those who reside and/or plan to recreate in the Watch\nareas.","ends":"2026-09-28T15:15:00-06:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"Archuleta, CO; Dolores, CO; Gunnison, CO; Hinsdale, CO; La Plata, CO; Mesa, CO; Montezuma, CO; Montrose, CO; Ouray, CO; San Juan, CO; San Miguel, CO; Grand, UT; San Juan, UT","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.7d95a2138492d35262c0408a70acac4faa29c1f7.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.7d95a2138492d35262c0408a70acac4faa29c1f7.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued September 27 at 10:27AM MDT until September 30 at 6:00AM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...Portions of Colorado, including the following areas, Animas\nRiver Basin, Four Corners/Upper Dolores River, Northwest San Juan\nMountains, Paradox Valley/Lower Dolores River, San Juan River\nBasin, Southwest San Juan Mountains and Uncompahgre Plateau and\nDallas Divide and southeast Utah, including the following areas,\nCanyonlands/Natural Bridges, La Sal and Abajo Mountains and\nSoutheast Utah.\n\n* WHEN...From Monday morning through late Tuesday night.\n\n* IMPACTS...Flooding will be possible over burn scars, at low-water\ncrossings, and along creeks, streams, washes, arroyos and other\nflood-prone locations. Creeks and streams may rise out of their\nbanks. Unpaved roads could become muddy and impassable.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Periods of heavy rainfall, associated with remnant moisture from\nHurricane Polo, are expected to produce numerous showers and\nembedded thunderstorms. Heavy rain falling on saturated soils and\nburn scars could lead to localized flash flooding and rises on\nlocal rivers.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-09-30T06:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Uncompahgre Plateau/Dallas Divide; Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains; Paradox Valley/Lower Dolores River; Four Corners/Upper Dolores River; Animas River Basin; San Juan River Basin; Southeast Utah; La Sal and Abajo Mountains; Canyonlands/Natural Bridges","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.4e0b211dcd7bab58026a9cb01526038e869174d9.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.4e0b211dcd7bab58026a9cb01526038e869174d9.001.1"},{"event":"Hydrologic Outlook","headline":"Hydrologic Outlook issued September 25 at 2:21PM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"ESFGJT\n\nIn the wake of today's precipitation, our attention will turn to the\nEastern Pacific. Tropical moisture is anticipated to spread across\nthe Desert Southwest early next week, drawing moisture from a pair of\nHurricanes; Odalys and Polo. The forecast becomes increasing complex\ndue to a closed low currently forecast to drive across the Southwest\nCONUS as Hurricane Polo approaches the Baja California Peninsula.\nStrong southerly winds ahead of the low are expected to pull tropical\nmoisture towards eastern Utah and western Colorado as early as Monday\nmorning. The challenge and uncertainty in our precipitation forecast\nincrease as the week progresses and the low traverses our southern\nStates. Excessive rainfall is most probable over AZ and NM, attm, but\nthe northerly extent poses a threat to our southern San Juan river\nbasins. Current forecasts suggests 1 to 3 inches of rain over Monday\nmorning through Wednesday. At face value, rivers and tributaries will\nsee a bump up due to said moisture. However, localized heavier\namounts could result in minor flooding across the southern San Juan\nfoothills and communities further downstream.\n\nRivers have responded rapidly to recent rainfall due to saturated\nsoils and increased runoff potential. Thus, prolonged periods of\nadditional rainfall over these regions would trigger a similar\nresponse. The current precipitation forecast suggests larger peaks in\nseveral creeks and rivers than what has been observed over the\nprevious 7 days. However, high end probabilistic rainfall amounts\n(i.e. above 90th percentile) may lead to rivers exceeding action\nstage to minor flooding. Most probable basins to see significant\nrises include; the Rio Blanco, San Juan, Piedra, Los Pinos/Vallecito\nCreek, and the Animas River Basins.\n\nConvective storms and periods of heavy rainfall fed from said\ntropical moisture could result in localized flooding impacts across\nthe CWA, however, confidence decreases rapidly north of the San\nJuans. Please continue to monitor the forecast as these systems\nevolve. Especially for those who reside and/or plan to recreate in\nthe southwest quadrant of the forecast area.","ends":"2026-09-30T06:00:00-06:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"Archuleta, CO; Dolores, CO; Gunnison, CO; Hinsdale, CO; La Plata, CO; Montezuma, CO; Ouray, CO; San Juan, CO; San Miguel, CO; Grand, UT; San Juan, UT","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.ce67801ec73048f07c44dd0418c6a50edd1c8fdc.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.ce67801ec73048f07c44dd0418c6a50edd1c8fdc.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-09-27T23:59:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KGJT 272359 AFDGJT Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Grand Junction CO 559 PM MDT Sun Sep 27 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Warmer and drier conditions continue today. - Increased moisture will arrive by Monday, bringing widespread moderate to heavy precipitation through midweek. - Chances of prolonged rainfall increases flooding concerns in southeast Utah and southwest Colorad","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=AFD&format=CI"},"hwo":null,"fwf":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-09-27T19:37:00+00:00","snippet":".DISCUSSION... A few isolated showers and thunderstorms are possible this afternoon, mostly over the high terrain. This evening showers are expected over the central and northern Divide. A strong push of moisture will allow for widespread precipitation Monday and Tuesday. Prolonged rainfall is possible, which increases concerns of flooding, particularly in southeast Utah and southwest Colorado. Th","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=FWF&format=CI"},"coagmet":null,"aviation":{"icao":"KGUC","distance_km":35.3,"raw_metar":"METAR KGUC 272356Z AUTO 11010KT 10SM BKN085 19/06 A3024 RMK AO2 SLP175 T01890061 10222 20172 56007 $","temp_f":66,"wind_dir":110,"wind_kt":10,"gust_kt":null,"visibility":"10+","cover":"BKN","flight_category":"VFR","altimeter":1024.1,"observed":1790553360,"raw_taf":"TAF KGUC 272338Z 2800/2824 VRB06KT P6SM SCT070 BKN130 OVC200 FM280800 05004KT P6SM OVC080 PROB30 2810/2812 4SM -SHRA BKN050 FM281200 VRB03KT P6SM -SHRA OVC050 FM281500 VRB05KT 6SM -SHRA BKN025 OVC040 FM281800 11010KT 4SM -SHRA BKN015 OVC045","url":"https://aviationweather.gov/data/metar/?ids=KGUC&hours=0"},"airgradient":null,"airnow":{"aqi":13,"category":"Good","parameter":"PM2.5","reporting_area":"Telluride","observed":"2026-09-27 17","by_parameter":{"PM2.5":{"aqi":13,"category":"Good","reporting_area":"Telluride","observed":"2026-09-27 17"},"PM10":{"aqi":9,"ca